{{Quote|Along the old Koros Trunk Line (codenamed "Nexus Route"), along which the ancients once used to transport carbonite from Koros Major to Coruscant.|Commander Neyo|<ref name="OSWM 123">{{GermanFanCite|123|Database}}</ref>}}
The '''Koros Trunk Line''', later also known as the '''Carbonite Pipeline''', was a [[hyperspace route]] that started at the [[Galaxy|galactic]] [[capital]] of [[Coruscant]] in the [[Core Worlds]] and ended at the [[planet]] [[Empress Teta]] in the [[Deep Core]], all falling within [[the Interior]] region of [[the galaxy]].

It was formed prior to the establishment of the [[Galactic Republic]], supposedly by a [[Coruscanti]] family on a [[generation ship]], and was used to transport [[carbonite]] from Empress Teta to Coruscant. The route made up part of the secret [[Nexus Route]] which the [[Confederacy of Independent Systems Navy|Separatist Fleet]] used to [[Battle of Coruscant|attack]] Coruscant in [[19 BBY]].

==Description==
The Koros Trunk Line was a [[hyperspace route]]<ref name="Nexus" /> located in [[the Interior]] region of [[the galaxy]].<ref name="Interior" /> It began<ref name="Nexus" /> in the [[Core Worlds]] region with the [[Galaxy|galactic]] [[capital]] of [[Coruscant]],<ref name="BMF 36" /> located in the [[Coruscant system]] of the [[Corusca sector]]'s [[Coruscant subsector]].<ref name="CGttSWG" /> From there, it entered the [[Deep Core]] region and passed through the [[Jerrilek system]]<ref name="BMF 36" /> and astronomical object [[Nacon]]<ref name="Galaxy Map" /> before ending at [[Empress Teta]], a [[carbonite]]-rich [[planet]] in the [[Koros sector]]'s [[Empress Teta system]] that was originally known as Koros Major or just Koros, from which the route took its name.<ref name="Nexus" /> The route also later became known as the Carbonite Pipeline,<ref name="BMF 36" /> as it was used to transport carbonite to Coruscant from Empress Teta.<ref name="OSWM 123" />

Empress Teta acted as a junction from the route onto the [[Byss Run]] and the [[Carbonite Run]], while Coruscant connected it to the [[Corellian Run]], [[Metellos Trade Route]], [[Namadii Corridor]], [[Perlemian Trade Route]], and [[Stassia Alley]].<ref name="Junctions" /> The Koros Trunk Line formed a terminal portion of the [[Nexus Route]], which also terminated at Coruscant and included the Byss Run and a portion of the [[Rimma Trade Route]].<ref name="NexusRoute">[[Star Wars Galaxy Map (poster)|''Star Wars'' Galaxy Map poster]] shows what {{BuildFalconCite|36|Guide to the Galaxy}} identifies as the Koros Trunk Line as part of a larger route that "[[The Citadel (episode)|The Citadel]]" identifies as the [[Nexus Route]].</ref> The Koros Trunk Line occupied grid squares L-9 and L-10 on the [[Standard Galactic Grid]].<ref name="Galaxy Map" />

==History==
After Coruscant had become an established world but before the [[Galactic Republic]] had formed,<ref name="BMF 36" /> the Koros Trunk Line was blazed, allegedly by a [[Coruscanti]] [[Unidentified Coruscanti family|family]] on a [[generation ship]]. A carbonite statue of the family's [[Unidentified Coruscanti patriarch|patriarch]] stood as a [[monument to lost navigators]] in front of the [[Hyperspace Navigator's Guildhouse]] on Empress Teta.<ref name="Nexus" /> The route was used to bring carbonite from Empress Teta, which was known as Koros Major at the time, to the world of Coruscant. However, over time the route fell into obscurity.<ref name="OSWM 123" />

During the [[Clone Wars]], [[Supreme Chancellor]] [[Darth Sidious|Sheev Palpatine]] of the Republic&mdash;the public identity of the [[Sith Lord]] Darth Sidious&mdash;provided the coordinates for the secret Nexus Route to [[Confederacy of Independent Systems]] [[General]] [[Grievous]]. The general then led the [[Confederacy of Independent Systems Navy|Separatist Fleet]] from the [[Outer Rim Territories]] down the length of the route to reach Coruscant and launch a [[Battle of Coruscant|surprise attack]] on the Republic capital<ref name="CloneWars">{{EncyclopediaCite|The End of the Clone Wars}}</ref> in [[19 BBY]].<ref name="Timelines">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]''</ref> The hyperspace lane was mentioned in "[[Neyo's Almanac of Clankers]]," a survival compendium sent out by [[Clone Marshal Commander|Commander]] [[Neyo]], and described the history of how ancient people used the lane. He mentioned how Grievous used the Nexus Route, of which the Koros Trunk Line was a portion of, and questioned how the Separatists got the coordinates.<ref name="OSWM 123" />

[[File:Empress_Teta_FDNP.png|thumb|left|250px|The Koros Trunk Line took its name from Koros, more commonly known as Empress Teta.]]
During the [[Imperial Era|reign]] of the [[Galactic Empire]], Palpatine&mdash;now [[Galactic Emperor]]&mdash;ordered that only [[starship]]s authorized by the [[Bureau of Ships and Services]] or the [[Imperial Security Bureau]] would be able to enter the Deep Core. [[Hyperspace]] jumps were limited to two [[star system]]s at a [[time]], after which travelers would have to check in with Imperial vessels and download updated astrogation data. This was enforced through the [[Hyperspace Security Net]] of [[Gravity mine|gravitic mines]] along hyperlanes and [[interdictor]]s at key points. Publicly it was claimed that this was to protect travelers from the violent anomalies found within the densely packed region, where even charted routes were dangerous to navigate due to the ever-shifting nature of the many [[star]]s, but it also served the purpose of protecting various secrets that the [[Emperor]] held within the Deep Core.<ref name="BMF 36" />

In [[34 ABY]],<ref name="Starcruiserdate">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]'' dates the events of [[Star Wars: Galactic Starcruiser|''Star Wars'': Galactic Starcruiser]] to [[34 ABY]].</ref> [[Chandrila Star Line]]'s [[MPO-1400 Purgill-class star cruiser|Star Cruiser]] ''[[Halcyon]]'' displayed a partial map of the galaxy that showed the Koros Trunk Line on several [[Viewscreen|screens]] while on a cruise from the Core Worlds' [[Chandrila system]] to the Outer Rim Territories.<ref name="GS">[[Star Wars: Galactic Starcruiser|''Star Wars'': Galactic Starcruiser]]</ref>

==Behind the scenes==
In the current ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[canon]], the Koros Trunk Line was first mentioned in the [[Star Wars: Build the Millennium Falcon 36|thirty-sixth]] issue of the ''[[Star Wars: Build the Millennium Falcon]]'' magazine,<ref name="BMF 36" /> published around [[September 9]], [[2015]].<ref name="BMFdate">{{BuildFalconDates|36}}</ref> The route's first [[in-universe]] appearance came in the form of being depicted on a map viewable in [[Star Wars: Galactic Starcruiser|''Star Wars'': Galactic Starcruiser]], an immersive roleplaying experience at [[W:c:disney:Walt Disney World Resort|Walt Disney World Resort]] in Florida<ref name="GS" /> that opened to the public on [[March 1]], [[2022]],<ref name="31 Things">{{SW|url=news/31-things-we-learned-about-star-wars-galactic-starcruiser|text=31 Things We Learned in an Exclusive Preview of ''Star Wars'': Galactic Starcruiser|nolive=1}}</ref> and closed on [[September 30]], [[2023]].<ref name="Official website">{{Disney|subdomain=disneyworld|domain=go.com|url=star-wars-galactic-starcruiser/overview|text=''Star Wars'': Galactic Starcruiser - Overview}}</ref>

The [[Koros Trunk Line/Legends|Koros Trunk Line]] originated in the [[Star Wars Legends|''Star Wars'' Legends]] continuity, where it was first mentioned in ''[[Coruscant and the Core Worlds]]'', a [[2003]] supplement for the ''[[Star Wars Roleplaying Game]]'' published by [[Wizards of the Coast]].<ref name="CatCW">''[[Coruscant and the Core Worlds]]''</ref> The name Carbonite Pipeline was then introduced in "Byss and the Deep Core
Part 2: Empress Teta," a [[2005]] ''Star Wars Roleplaying Game'' article written by [[Rodney Thompson]] and also published by Wizards of the Coast.<ref name="EmpressTeta">{{Wizards|url=default.asp?x=starwars/article/sw20050414planet2|series=Byss and the Deep Core|text=Part 2: Empress Teta}}</ref> It was then pictured for the first time in ''[[The Essential Atlas]]'', a [[2009]] reference book written by [[Daniel Wallace]] and [[Jason Fry]].<ref name="TEA">''[[The Essential Atlas]]''</ref>

